{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792601903,"nonce":"PbUV4TSahpQPj3a5SQKQ/XqyTxk=","title":"Native CLIs: reduce ELF loader startup without narrowing compatibility","message":"Follow-up to the TypeScript small-program startup profile bbb12fa75ae0ba87b2c0d0f371b92c45712c3d781a84308f77bc073e08160b3d. Frozen NUC release binaries show meaningful process cost before any runtime is created: --version was 3.38 ms on the previous HIR-cache binary, 3.79 ms on the initial worker binary and 3.46 ms after adding phase instrumentation/relinking. Minor faults were 785, 856 and 832 respectively, no major faults. Compiler/native setup cannot account for all of these build-to-build changes.\n\nAll variants link the same native dependencies and contain about 402 .init_array entries. The lazy-target variant still has about 1.7 MB of .rela.dyn relocation records. A linker-only experiment, cargo rustc --release -p zyntax_ts --bin zynode --features llvm-backend -- -C link-arg=-Wl,-z,pack-relative-relocs (same RUSTFLAGS -L /home/raikage/llvmshim), shrinks relative relocation storage into .relr.dyn (14,288 bytes; remaining .rela.dyn 99,888 bytes). It adds GLIBC_ABI_DT_RELR to the executable's required ABI. Do not enable it globally without establishing supported loader/deployment baselines.\n\nDiagnostic randomized 21 samples per binary/mode (315 child processes), CPUs 0-7, no compiler jobs before samples. Tracing enabled, so these are diagnostic comparisons, not the authoritative untraced benchmark. Eager / lazy / lazy+RELR median wall ms:\n- --version: 3.965 / 3.526 / 3.315; minor faults 874 / 857 / 813.\n- tiny uncached LLVM selected, interpreter-only: 6.042 / 4.844 / 4.846.\n- tiny warm HIR: 5.732 / 4.712 / 4.517.\n- imported warm HIR: 7.120 / 6.134 / 5.873.\n- tiny LLVM disabled: 5.589 / 4.703 / 4.646.\nNo major faults. Runtime trace eager LLVM setup ~0.28 ms -\u003e lazy ~0.02 ms; total runtime creation ~0.57 -\u003e ~0.26 ms. The larger whole-process difference includes ELF layout/startup effects and cannot all be credited to lazy target creation.\n\nProfile relocation processing and native static constructors separately; compare identical source/flags across repeat links. Evaluate modern-Linux packaging or an opt-in link configuration before changing minimum ABI. Check Python/Lua/ZynML as well: this is a linked-native-executable concern, not a TS semantic/frontend transformation. Preserve PIE/ASLR and interpreter-first tiering. Do not introduce a frontend-specific cache format or tune thresholds to hide the cost.\n\nReports: /private/tmp/ts-small-profile.json and /private/tmp/ts-small-relr-profile.json; same filenames under /home/raikage on the NUC. Experimental binaries /home/raikage/ts-small-{profile-phases,lazy,relr}-zynode. RELR is an experiment only; no repository linker flags were changed. Final lazy-target source was subsequently adjusted to initialize the target/LLVM options before IR construction; final measurements are recorded on the parent profile issue.","files":null}]}